-- title:
--   Theorem 4.11 — exact settlement and individual rationality of the one-sided auction
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be an admissible cost on $[0,\mathrm{cap})$ with initial right derivative $p_\star$, and consider any completed run of the one-sided clinching auction it generates, on a profile of finite nonnegative reports. The run is exact-settlement feasible:
--   $$0\le X_f<\mathrm{cap},\qquad Y_f=K(X_f),\qquad 0\le y_i\le q_i,\ x_i\ge0\ \text{for every identity}.$$
--   On a truthful profile it is individually rational: for every identity $i$ with value $V_i$ and budget $Q_i$,
--   $$0\le y_i\le Q_i,\qquad V_ix_i-y_i\ge0.\qquad(41)$$
--
--   For $K=C$, $p_\star=p_0$, $\mathrm{cap}=x_0$ this is the paper's statement for the one-sided AMM auction.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: the one-sided AMM clinching auction on the pool cost C (initial price p0, capacity x0) → the auction chordMech K pstar cap of any cost K with IsAdmissibleCost K pstar cap; reason: Lemma 6.1 states that the proof uses only these cost properties and applies the result to C̃_M and C̃^rev_M; C on [0, x0) with pstar = p0 is an admissible cost, so the Lean statement specializes to the source's
--   - reading: continuous evolution by the primitive conditions (16)–(18) on event-free open intervals, on which the clock and state paths are absolutely continuous → IsRun: the event procedure is applied at every clock price; off Clinch the continuous part of dx_i has no mass; a clincher stays at the water level on a right neighbourhood of every price while active; no absolute-continuity requirement on the paths; reason: event prices may accumulate (lumps of an admissible target can be dense) and, for an admissible AMM whose right marginal ask is flat on a fat Cantor set, the sole-survivor path X = Z(p) is continuous but not absolutely continuous, so a literal AC requirement would admit no run; on event-free intervals with AC paths the two formulations coincide
--   - reading: reported values (v_i, q_i) ∈ ℝ²≥0 in the one-sided analysis → values in WithTop ℝ with the hypothesis v_i ≠ ⊤ for every identity; reason: the paper states that its one-sided analysis allows only finite-value reports (p.28); infinite values are the subject of Lemma 6.2
-- source:
--   Zhang, Li, Shi, Zhang, Xia, Han, Sybil-Proofness of Generalized Adaptive Clinching and Implications for Blockchain Mechanism Design, arXiv:2610.36629 (held copy sha256 93c5d05b…), §4.5, Theorem 4.11, p.19 (PDF p.21); (9), p.9 (PDF p.11)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_SybilClinch_IsRun
import Definitions.Def_SybilClinch_IsAdmissibleCost
import Definitions.Def_SybilClinch_chordMech
import Definitions.Def_SybilClinch_OneSidedProfile_Valid
import Definitions.Def_SybilClinch_Run_alloc
import Definitions.Def_SybilClinch_Run_payOf

set_option autoImplicit false

open Filter Topology Set MeasureTheory

namespace SybilClinch

/-- Zhang, Li, Shi, Zhang, Xia, Han, *Sybil-Proofness of Generalized Adaptive Clinching and Implications for Blockchain Mechanism Design*, arXiv:2610.36629 (held copy sha256 93c5d05b…), §4.5, Theorem 4.11 (Exact settlement and individual rationality), printed p.19 (PDF p.21),
with (9), printed p.9 (PDF p.11).

Every completed one-sided AMM clinching run is exact-settlement feasible: `0 ≤ X_f < cap`,
`Y_f = K(X_f)`, and every identity satisfies (1): `0 ≤ yᵢ ≤ qᵢ`, `xᵢ ≥ 0`. On a truthful profile it
is individually rational: `0 ≤ yᵢ ≤ Qᵢ` and `Vᵢxᵢ − yᵢ ≥ 0` (41).

**Formalization Note.** Stated for the auction generated by any admissible cost `K` on `[0, cap)`
with initial right derivative `p⋆` (generalization; Lemma 6.1 says the proof uses only these
properties). The paper's case is `K = C`, `p⋆ = p₀`, `cap = x₀`. Values are finite; a truthful
profile reads each report as the identity's true type. -/
theorem theorem_4_11 (K : ℝ → ℝ) (pstar cap : ℝ) (hK : IsAdmissibleCost K pstar cap)
    (P : OneSidedProfile) (hP : P.Valid) (hfin : ∀ i ∈ P.ids, P.v i ≠ ⊤) (R : Run)
    (hR : IsRun (chordMech K pstar cap) P R) :
    (0 ≤ R.alloc P.ids R.pf ∧ R.alloc P.ids R.pf < cap ∧
      R.payOf (chordMech K pstar cap) P.ids R.pf = K (R.alloc P.ids R.pf) ∧
      ∀ i ∈ P.ids, 0 ≤ R.pay (chordMech K pstar cap) i R.pf ∧
        R.pay (chordMech K pstar cap) i R.pf ≤ P.q i ∧ 0 ≤ R.x i R.pf) ∧
    ∀ i ∈ P.ids, ∀ Vi : ℝ, P.v i = (Vi : WithTop ℝ) →
      0 ≤ R.pay (chordMech K pstar cap) i R.pf ∧ R.pay (chordMech K pstar cap) i R.pf ≤ P.q i ∧
        0 ≤ Vi * R.x i R.pf - R.pay (chordMech K pstar cap) i R.pf := by sorry

end SybilClinch
